commit a798b637fd797fc6552f785e4ee1be08442b397c
Author: Avijit Pal <palavijit713130@gmail.com>
Date:   Sun Aug 9 08:24:11 2026 +0000

    refactor(backend): verify Prisma delegate names at compile time, remove the any budget
    
    Two pieces of debt tracked since the initial audit.
    
    1. The `no-explicit-any` warning cap is gone (22 -> 0)
    
       The audit framed this as "241 uses of any". That was the symptom. The
       disease was that twelve files each defined their own
       `prismaAny(): Record<string, any>` and indexed into it with a string,
       with the delegate and field names re-declared by hand in SEVEN
       separate maps — analytics, collections (twice), interaction, journal,
       library and progress. Nothing tied any of them to the generated
       client, so a wrong name produced no compile error and no runtime error
       either: the lookup returned undefined and the caller silently skipped
       that media type.
    
       That is exactly how `userId_mediaId` (a compound key that exists on no
       model) and a library update whitelist naming ten non-existent columns
       both shipped.
    
       Added common/media-types.ts as the single source of truth, with a
       compile-time assertion that every delegate name is a key of
       PrismaClient. A model rename in schema.prisma now fails the build
       instead of quietly disabling a feature. common/prisma-delegates.ts
       provides typed accessors over it and THROWS on an unknown name rather
       than returning null, so a typo surfaces immediately instead of
       becoming missing data.
    
       Applied where it removes real risk:
       - library.repository.ts fully migrated: zero `any`, no blanket
         disable, and the `userId_mediaId` bug fixed — it is now derived from
         the config as `userId_movieId` and friends.
       - verifyMediaExists took `type` straight from the request body and
         used it to index the Prisma client. It now resolves through the
         allowlist, so an unexpected value cannot reach a delegate by name.
       - search.repository.ts built delegate names by string concatenation
         (`user${Type}`), which was ungreppable and produced a silent
         `continue` — that media type simply absent from every search result
         — for any name it got wrong.
    
       Then cleared the remaining 21 warnings, all small and independent:
       Express.Multer.File instead of `any` for uploads, `unknown` in catch
       clauses, inferred controller return types, a typed subset of the
       Google profile, and the Nest logger instead of console in main.ts.
       Declared `Express` as a global in the eslint config — the ambient
       namespace from @types/express was reported as no-undef.
    
       CI lint gate tightened from --max-warnings=22 to 0.
    
       Not done, deliberately: the remaining files keep their file-level
       disable. Forcing `Record<string, unknown>` return types out of a
       runtime-selected delegate pushes the errors into the service layer,
       which then needs its own row interfaces — a multi-day refactor across
       24 files with real regression risk. The names are now verified, which
       was the actual hazard; the row typing is bounded follow-up work.
    
    2. Check-then-act ownership guards (16 sites, all converted)
    
       Every one followed the same shape: read the row, compare userId in
       JavaScript, then write with `where: { id }` and no owner scoping. Two
       costs — a window in which the row could change between check and
       write, and an extra query on every call.
    
       All 16 now fold ownership into the write predicate and branch on the
       returned count, across notifications, journal (7), collections (4),
       interaction, progress and library.
    
       The collection and shelf deletes needed more than a mechanical swap.
       Both removed child rows and the parent in an array-form
       `$transaction`, where a zero-row parent delete is not an error — so
       passing another user's id would have destroyed their items while their
       collection survived. Both now verify ownership inside an interactive
       transaction and abort. This is the same hazard I hit and fixed in
       deleteWrappedYear during Phase 2; it existed in two more places.
    
    Tests: updated the library and interaction specs, which asserted on the
    findUnique that no longer happens. They now assert the outcome and that
    the write is scoped by id AND userId, which is the property that
