commit c6709c67bd35f03a158a0e979bc771492b757d0f
Author: Avijit Pal <palavijit713130@gmail.com>
Date:   Sun Aug 9 07:26:33 2026 +0000

    feat: make the product tell the truth — persist writes, real insights, working password reset
    
    Four problems, all variations on the same theme: the UI presented things
    as real that were not.
    
    1. Every user write was discarded
       Status changes, favourites, progress, reflections and rewatch counts
       all went to localStorage via the Zustand store. Reads came from the
       API. The two never met, so anything a user recorded vanished on
       logout, on a browser-data clear, and on every other device — while
       the UI confirmed each action with a success toast.
    
       Added src/lib/store/useLibrarySync.ts and wired ProgressLogger,
       ReflectionDrawer and ItemActionBar through it. The store is kept as an
       optimistic cache rather than removed: it is synchronous, ~40
       components read it directly, and it is what makes the UI feel
       instant. Writes now go local-first for immediate feedback and then
       persist; on failure the user is told rather than left believing it
       saved.
    
       AddSheet is the exception and now says so. POST /library requires a
       mediaId that already exists in the catalog, but that sheet creates
       free-text entries. Rather than silently pretend, the toast reads
       "saved on this device". Wiring it properly needs a catalog search step
       or an endpoint that accepts user-authored media.
    
    2. Four endpoints the frontend called did not exist
       /analytics/discovery, /intelligence, /challenges and /constellation
       have been called since launch and always 404'd, so roughly ten
       dashboard, discovery and calendar sections rendered permanently
       empty. TypeScript could not catch it: the client declared return types
       for routes nobody had written.
    
       New DiscoveryService implements all four from the user's own library.
       Where there is not enough data to say something true it returns empty
       collections — a new user sees nothing rather than invented content,
       which is the same standard applied to the frontend below.
    
    3. Password reset was a stub that lied
       POST /auth/forgot-password added the address to a Redis set and
       returned "If an account exists, a reset link has been sent." No email
       was sent, no token was issued, and there was no endpoint to complete
       the flow. Anyone who forgot their password was permanently locked out
       while being told otherwise. The PasswordResetToken model has been in
       the schema, unused, the entire time.
    
       Implemented end to end: 256-bit token stored only as a SHA-256 hash,
       30-minute expiry, single-use enforced atomically via a filtered
       updateMany so two concurrent redemptions cannot both succeed, and a
       new /auth/reset-password page. Requesting a reset still cannot be used
       to enumerate accounts — unknown, deleted and known addresses are
       indistinguishable, and expired, used and unknown tokens all return one
       message. Completing a reset revokes every refresh token, session and
       access token, because a reset is often a response to compromise and
       leaving the attacker's session alive would defeat it.
    
       Extended the email transport for reset mail and removed the dead
       logForgotPasswordRequest stub.
    
    4. Fabricated content presented as the user's own data
       - InsightCard cycled three hardcoded sentences under an "Insight"
         label ("You've watched more sci-fi this month than the past year
         combined"). Now renders statements from /analytics/intelligence,
         each with the evidence it was derived from, and nothing at all when
         there is nothing true to say.
       - RecommendationInsights showed five fixed lines under "What Avuno
         notices", two of which claimed knowledge of time-of-day and mood
         that the app does not collect.
       - LifetimeMilestones rendered a fixed 2021-2026 history, so a user who
         signed up yesterday was told they had a five-year past. Now one
         entry per year they were actually active.
       - ComfortStories fell back to Spirited Away, The Office, Stardew
         Valley and Harry Potter with invented rewatch counts, shown to users
         with empty libraries as their own comfort viewing.
       - The calendar aggregation sent every user "Your peak activity
         occurred in July" and "Sci-Fi was your most revisited genre" as
         literal strings, plus hardcoded streak values of 5 and 4. The peak
         month is now computed; the genre claim is gone, because that
         aggregation has no genre data.
    
       Removed six nav entries — Museum, Characters, Creators, Franchises,
       Goals, Achievements — whose index pages render a permanent "Coming
       soon" and whose $id pages always throw notFound(). The routes stay
       reachable by URL so the work is not lost, but a nav full of dead ends
       reads as a broken product.
    
    Tests: 28 new cases. DiscoveryService is covered for both a populated
    and an empty library, asserting explicitly that an empty library produces
    zero fabricated output. PasswordResetService is covered for replay,
    expiry, enumeration resistance and session revocation.
    
    Backend 597 passing, frontend 9 files. All gates green on both
    workspaces.

