commit 65b49d258a2f928febad059e807bfc66319df0ab
Author: Avijit Pal <palavijit713130@gmail.com>
Date:   Sun Aug 9 04:39:50 2026 +0000

    fix: five defects found reviewing the Phase 0 and Phase 1 commits
    
    Adversarial self-review of the 18 preceding commits. Three of these are
    mine; two are pre-existing bugs the review surfaced.
    
    1. HIGH — JWT without a `sub` claim passed the guard (mine, P1.2)
       JwtAuthGuard verified the signature, revocation and account state, but
       never checked that `sub` was actually present. A token without one
       reached handlers with `request.user.sub === undefined`, and because
       Prisma OMITS undefined fields from a WHERE clause, every
       `where: { id, userId }` ownership check silently degraded to
       `where: { id }` — quietly undoing the IDOR fixes from the previous
       commit. Not directly exploitable (forging a token needs the signing
       secret), but any token minted by another service sharing that secret
       would have disabled authorization entirely. Now rejected.
    
    2. MEDIUM — redactUrl had four bypasses (mine, P1.9)
       Verified by probing it rather than reading it. Credentials survived
       redaction when the parameter name was percent-encoded (`%74oken`),
       array-suffixed (`token[]`), prefixed with `+` or whitespace, or when
       it sat in the URL fragment. None of these occur on our own routes, but
       a redaction helper that can be sidestepped is not worth having. Key
       normalisation now decodes, trims and strips the array suffix, and
       fragments are redacted too. Six evasion cases added to the spec.
    
    3. MEDIUM — Docker could pair mismatched Prisma client and runtime (mine, P1/P0)
       The runtime image took node_modules from the `deps` stage but the
       generated Prisma client from `builder`. Both stages run independent
       `npm install`s (not `npm ci`, and no lockfile is copied), so they can
       resolve different @prisma/client patch versions; pairing a client
       generated against one version with a runtime of another fails at
       startup with an opaque engine error. `deps` now generates its own
       client via `npx --yes prisma@<version-from-that-tree>`, and the
       cross-stage copies are gone.
    
       Confirmed while checking: argon2 ships an `argon2.musl.node` prebuild
       inside node_modules, so copying the tree into the toolchain-free
       Alpine runner does work.
    
    4. MEDIUM — session cleanup deleted the wrong rows (pre-existing)
       cleanup.processor.ts deleted sessions where `status: 'ACTIVE'` and
       `updatedAt` older than 90 days — reaping LIVE sessions for anyone who
       had not used the app in three months, while REVOKED and EXPIRED rows
       accumulated forever. The filter was inverted. Now prunes finished
       sessions and anything past its expiry.
    
    5. LOW — replaced an `any` in the reorderItems transaction with a narrow
       structural type, so the query shapes stay checked.
    
    Also verified, no change needed:
     - The OAuth flow rejects all three attacks end-to-end (no state, forged
       state, replayed state): zero credentials issued, no token in the URL.
       Confirmed at the library level that passport-oauth2 forwards a string
       `options.state` verbatim and that NullStore.verify is a no-op, so our
       controller is the sole verifier and runs before any credential exists.
     - Legacy scrypt hashes still verify and upgrade cleanly to argon2id.
     - No remaining plaintext reads of sessions.token anywhere in the backend.
     - Both bun lockfiles satisfy --frozen-lockfile, so CI installs will
       succeed on a clean checkout.
     - The release gate's check-name matches the launch-gate job name exactly,
       and CI runs on `v*` tags, so the wait cannot deadlock. The concurrency
       group keys on ref, so a tag push does not cancel the branch run.
    
    New: src/lib/api/fetch-concurrency.test.ts — six behavioural traces for
    the auth/retry loop covering five simultaneous 401s sharing a single
    refresh, external abort propagation, method-dependent 429 handling, and
    204 responses.
    
    Gates: frontend and backend both fully green (backend 553 tests passing).

