commit cba2d9f556eee243d0397fcab1c4fb82d29b2fca
Author: Avijit Pal <palavijit713130@gmail.com>
Date:   Sun Aug 9 01:20:04 2026 +0000

    fix(security): move rate limiting to Redis and stop the lockout DoS
    
    Four related weaknesses in the abuse-prevention layer.
    
    1. Rate limits were per-process and reset on every deploy
       ThrottlerModule was configured with no storage, so @nestjs/throttler
       fell back to an in-memory Map. With N API replicas the effective limit
       was N x limit, and every restart wiped every counter — so the limits
       protecting register (3/min), login (5/min), refresh (10/min) and
       forgot-password (3/min) were far weaker than they appear, and an
       attacker could reset them by triggering a redeploy.
    
       New RedisThrottlerStorage implements the ThrottlerStorage interface
       directly against the existing RedisService rather than pulling in
       another package for ~40 lines in the auth path. The app's own
       rate-limit-audit.service.ts already recommended exactly this.
    
       It fails OPEN: rate limiting is availability protection, not an
       authorization control, so a Redis outage must not become a total
       outage. The auth lockout below is the piece that fails closed.
    
    2. Any known email could be locked out indefinitely
       recordFailedAttempt called expire() on every attempt, sliding the
       window forward each time. A single request every 14 minutes kept an
       arbitrary account locked forever — a targeted denial of service
       against any address an attacker knows. The TTL is now set only when
       the counter is created (expire NX), so a lockout is bounded to
       LOCKOUT_MINUTES from the first failure.
    
    3. Redis accumulated plaintext email addresses
       Both the lockout counter and the forgot-password endpoint keyed on the
       raw address, so Redis became a plaintext list of everyone who had ever
       tried to sign in or reset. Both now key on a SHA-256 digest.
    
    4. forgot-password accepted unbounded unvalidated input
       It took `@Body('email') email: string`, which bypasses the global
       ValidationPipe entirely (no metatype to validate against), and wrote
       the value into an unbounded Redis SET with no TTL — an unauthenticated
       memory-exhaustion vector. Now a ForgotPasswordDto with @IsEmail and
       @MaxLength(254), stored as a bounded per-address counter that expires
       after 24h. The response is unchanged and still identical whether or
       not the address is registered.
    
       Note the endpoint remains a stub: no email is sent and no reset token
       is issued. The PasswordResetToken model exists in the schema and is
       unused; implementing the flow is tracked separately. Documented in the
       method so it is not mistaken for working.
    
    Also: RATE_LIMIT_TTL and RATE_LIMIT_GLOBAL were read by app.module.ts but
    declared nowhere, so they were always undefined and silently fell back to
    the inline defaults. Both are now validated in env.validation.ts and
    documented in .env.example. Also made the ipKey nullable rather than
    bucketing all IP-less traffic under the literal 'unknown', where one bot
    could lock out every client without a resolvable address.
    
    Tests: 9 cases in redis-throttler.storage.spec.ts, including that the
    window is fixed rather than sliding, that blocks expire, that keys and
    throttlers are isolated, and that it fails open. Verified AppModule still
    boots with the storage wired. Backend src suite 512 -> 521 passing; the 3
    remaining failures are the pre-existing @nestjs/testing module-loading
    race, unchanged.

