commit 51f3cb670ce56cc2cd0dc33a8523c8137b91a97c
Author: Avijit Pal <palavijit713130@gmail.com>
Date:   Sat Aug 8 14:40:21 2026 +0000

    fix(auth): store session tokens hashed instead of in plaintext
    
    RefreshTokenService correctly stored only sha256(token). SessionService
    was then handed the same raw token and wrote it verbatim to
    sessions.token, which negated the hashing entirely — the sessions table
    held live, directly replayable refresh tokens for every active user.
    
    Anything that yields a read of that table hands over working credentials:
    a read-only SQL injection, a leaked replica, an unencrypted pg_dump (the
    backup service writes exactly that), or a stolen backup file. Hashing the
    other copy bought nothing while this one existed.
    
    Changes
    - New session-token.ts centralises hashSessionToken() and a constant-time
      sessionTokenMatches(). Plain SHA-256 rather than a password KDF is
      correct here: the input is an opaque 256-bit random token, so there is
      no guess space to brute-force, and lookups must remain a single indexed
      query.
    - SessionService hashes on create and on every lookup and invalidation.
    - RefreshTokenService.rotateWithSession hashes on both sides of the
      rotation, so the old session is still found and the new one is stored
      hashed.
    - UsersService.toSessionResponse compared session.token === refreshToken.
      That now hashes the incoming cookie first, and compares in constant
      time — a plain === would leak, via timing, how many leading characters
      matched.
    
    The column keeps its `token` name to avoid a rename migration; it now
    holds a digest.
    
    Migration (20260808_hash_session_tokens) revokes all active sessions and
    outstanding refresh tokens. Existing rows cannot be converted in place —
    hashing needs the plaintext, and the entire point is to stop holding it.
    Affected users sign in again, which is the right trade for removing live
    credentials from the database. Refresh tokens are revoked alongside so a
    rotation cannot resurrect a session that no longer exists.
    
    Tests: 10 cases in session-token.spec.ts, including that a legacy
    plaintext value left in the column does not match — otherwise the
    migration would be bypassable. Verified end-to-end that create() writes
    the hash and never the raw token, and that validate() and
    invalidateByToken() still resolve. Updated users.service.spec fixtures,
    which asserted against plaintext. Backend src suite 512 passing; the 3
    remaining failures are the pre-existing @nestjs/testing module-loading
    race, unchanged.

