commit c53d3e47d5c68534fb6932ed412415ffd77abfff
Author: Avijit Pal <palavijit713130@gmail.com>
Date:   Sat Aug 8 14:25:18 2026 +0000

    fix(security): close two IDOR holes in collections and journal
    
    Both let an authenticated user reach another user's rows. Every other
    query in these modules is user-scoped; these were the outliers.
    
    1. reorderItems rewrote arbitrary CollectionItem rows
       collections.repository.ts verified ownership of the *collection*, then
       issued updates with `where: { id: itemId }` and no collectionId:
    
           const queries = itemIds.map((itemId, i) =>
             collectionItem.update({ where: { id: itemId }, data: { position: i } }));
    
       So PATCH /collections/<my-collection>/items/reorder with a body of
       someone else's item IDs rewrote `position` across the table, scrambling
       their curated ordering. ReorderItemsDto validated only
       @IsArray + @IsString({each}), so the IDs were arbitrary unbounded
       strings and the array had no size cap — a single request could open a
       transaction of tens of thousands of writes.
    
       Now an interactive transaction verifies every ID belongs to the target
       collection *before* writing, so a batch containing a foreign ID is
       rejected outright rather than partially applied. DTO tightened to
       @IsUUID('4', {each}) with @ArrayNotEmpty and @ArrayMaxSize(500).
    
    2. Journal media lookup resolved any user's library items
       findLibraryMediaId took no userId and ran
       findUnique({ where: { id: libraryId } }). Reached from createQuote and
       createHighlight, so POST /library/<victim-item-id>/quotes?type=movie
       both confirmed that row exists (201 vs 404 is an existence oracle) and
       bound the victim's media into the attacker's own quote. The media loop
       in createMemory had the same flaw.
    
       All three now filter on { id, userId, deletedAt: null }.
    
       The `?type=` query param also selects a Prisma delegate at runtime and
       was a bare @Query('type') string, which bypasses the global
       ValidationPipe entirely. It is now a MediaTypeQueryDto with an @IsIn
       allowlist. The allowlist lives in a new journal.media-types.ts rather
       than in the service, because the DTOs validate against it and the
       service imports the DTOs — declaring it in the service created an
       import cycle.
    
    Also, while fixing the ownership scoping in createMemory: it resolved
    media with a nested loop, one findUnique per media ID per media type, all
    sequential. A memory with 20 items meant up to 160 round-trips in a
    single request. Now one batched findMany per type, run in parallel — 8
    queries regardless of item count — while preserving the caller's ordering.
    
    Tests: 11 cases across journal.idor.spec.ts and reorder-items.spec.ts.
    Both were verified against the vulnerable code first: reintroducing the
    journal flaw fails 3 of 6, reintroducing the collections flaw fails 4 of
    5. Backend src suite 471 -> 502 passing; the 3 remaining failures are the
    pre-existing @nestjs/testing module-loading race, unchanged.

