commit ef646f608bd62bc393ae34d7f2e8802861b9e11b
Author: Avijit Pal <palavijit713130@gmail.com>
Date:   Sat Aug 8 14:05:45 2026 +0000

    fix(security): make CORS fail closed instead of reflecting any origin
    
    The CORS origin callback had two fail-open branches, both combined with
    credentials: true:
    
        if (allowedOrigins.length === 0 ||      // unset/empty CORS_ORIGIN
            allowedOrigins.includes('*') ||     // explicit wildcard
            allowedOrigins.includes(origin))
          return callback(null, true);
    
    CORS_ORIGIN is not validated anywhere, and docker-compose.yml does not
    set it at all, so an empty value was entirely plausible in a real
    deployment. In that state the server reflected whatever Origin the caller
    sent, together with Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true — meaning any
    website could issue credentialed requests to the API with the victim's
    .avuno.xyz cookies and read the responses. POST /api/auth/refresh is the
    sharpest edge: an attacker's page could mint a fresh access token from a
    victim's refresh cookie and read it out of the response body.
    
    The wildcard branch is a related mistake. Browsers reject '*' alongside
    credentials, so honouring it server-side by reflecting the caller's
    Origin converts an invalid configuration into an open one.
    
    Changes
    - Parse CORS_ORIGIN once at boot rather than per request, so a bad value
      is a startup failure rather than a silent runtime behaviour.
    - Throw when CORS_ORIGIN contains '*' — it cannot be combined with
      credentialed requests, so it is always a misconfiguration.
    - Throw when CORS_ORIGIN is missing in production. In development, warn
      and reject cross-origin requests rather than allowing them.
    - The per-request check is now a plain allowlist test. Requests with no
      Origin header (same-origin, curl, server-to-server) still pass, since
      CORS does not apply to them.
    
