commit eea62ba90e19272a034318b18e1ca6da0bb6e389
Author: Avijit Pal <palavijit713130@gmail.com>
Date:   Sat Aug 8 13:59:32 2026 +0000

    fix(auth): switch to argon2id and remove the hardcoded OAuth encryption key
    
    Two credential-storage weaknesses, plus the test debt that was hiding
    them.
    
    1. Password hashing was scrypt at Node's defaults
       crypto.scrypt with no options object means N=16384, r=8, p=1 — about
       an eighth of the OWASP argon2id baseline and materially cheaper to
       attack with GPUs or ASICs. argon2 was already a declared dependency
       and simply unused; the existing spec even asserted '$argon2id$' and
       had been failing since it was written.
    
       Now argon2id at m=19456, t=2, p=1. Existing scrypt hashes still
       verify, and needsRehash() drives a transparent upgrade on the next
       successful login, so no password reset is required.
    
       Also fixed the legacy comparison itself: it called reject(err) without
       returning, then dereferenced an undefined derivedKey and threw inside
       the executor after already rejecting.
    
    2. Login leaked which emails are registered
       An unknown address returned in ~1ms because no hashing ran, while a
       known one took ~40ms. Uniform error messages do not help when the
       timing differs by 40x. login() now performs a dummy argon2
       verification on the unknown-user path; a test asserts the two are
       within the same order of magnitude.
    
    3. OAuth provider tokens were encrypted under a published key
       OAuthAccountRepository fell back to the literal
       'default_secret_key_32_bytes_long!', and configuration.ts fell back to
       'dev_only_secret_key_32_bytes_long_'. Both are in the public git
       history. The repository's production guard never fired, because
       configuration.ts had already substituted its own default upstream, so
       `secret` was never falsy. A deployment with NODE_ENV set to anything
       other than 'production' would AES-256-GCM encrypt every stored Google
       access and refresh token under a key anyone can read.
    
       Worse, the key was derived with padEnd(32,'0').slice(0,32), so a short
       key became low-entropy padding with no warning.
    
       Both fallbacks are gone. OAUTH_ENCRYPTION_KEY must now be 64 hex
       characters, validated at boot in env.validation.ts so a bad value
       fails immediately rather than at the first OAuth login. The local
       .env key was 32 hex characters (16 bytes) and has been regenerated.
    
       decrypt() also no longer swallows failures into console.warn. A GCM
       auth-tag mismatch is the signal for tampered ciphertext and now logs
       through the Nest logger with the reason; it still returns empty rather
       than throwing, because the same failure occurs legitimately after a
       key rotation and the caller treats it as "re-authorise with Google".
    
    Test debt cleared: the auth suite goes from 66 passing / 22 failing to
    119 passing / 0 failing. Fixing the mocks surfaced three real problems
    in the tests themselves:
    
      - Two `expect(...).rejects` assertions were not awaited, so they passed
        regardless of behaviour. Awaiting the duplicate-registration one
        revealed the mock repository did not enforce the email unique
        constraint, so it never exercised the P2002 path that AuthService
        actually relies on.
      - A test asserted login() returns refreshToken in its response body. It
        never has, and must not — the refresh token is httpOnly-cookie only.
        Now asserts the field is absent.
      - console-email-transport.spec asserted the raw verification token is
        logged. c995a15 deliberately removed that; the test was pinning a
        security regression. Now asserts it is NOT logged.
    
    Backend src suite: 471 -> 491 passing, 13 -> 3 failing. The remaining 3
    are a pre-existing @nestjs/testing module-loading race that only occurs
    when the whole suite shares one process; those files pass in isolation
    and fail identically on the unmodified tree.

