commit e58e7cd284704b92645a75d6d687ad2f381a0065
Author: Avijit Pal <palavijit713130@gmail.com>
Date:   Sat Aug 8 13:21:08 2026 +0000

    fix(auth): make access tokens revocable and enforce live account state
    
    Logging out did not end access. Access tokens are stateless JWTs and
    logout only revoked the refresh token, so the bearer credential kept
    working for its full lifetime (default 15 minutes). "Log out all
    devices" had the same gap: a user reporting a stolen device was told
    every session was ended while the attacker's token kept working.
    
    The same hole affected authorization. JwtAuthGuard trusted the token
    alone and never read the user record, so until natural expiry:
      - a demoted admin retained admin rights, including
        GET /api/users/admin/metrics
      - a suspended user retained full access
      - a soft-deleted user retained full access (no auth query filters
        deletedAt)
    
    Revocation
    - Access tokens now carry a `jti`. New TokenRevocationService denylists
      an individual token on logout, and records a per-user revocation epoch
      for logout-all, where the individual jti values are unknown. Both keys
      expire after the access-token lifetime, so the denylist stays bounded
      regardless of user count.
    - The epoch cutoff is now+1s because `iat` has one-second granularity; a
      token minted in the same second as the revocation would otherwise
      survive it. Covered by a test.
    - isRevoked fails CLOSED. An unreachable Redis is indistinguishable from
      an empty denylist, and silently reinstating every revoked session is
      the worse outcome. Write failures throw rather than reporting a
      successful logout that did not revoke anything.
    - /auth/logout stays unguarded so a user with an expired access token can
      still end their session; the bearer token is read opportunistically via
      tryDecodeAccessToken and denylisted when present and valid. It also now
      always clears the cookie, where previously the whole call was skipped
      when no refresh cookie was sent.
    
    Account state
    - New UserStateService reads status, role and deletedAt, cached in Redis
      for 30s so the guard does not add a query per request, with explicit
      invalidation on change (wired into email verification, which moves
      PENDING_VERIFICATION -> ACTIVE).
    - The guard rejects deleted and non-active accounts and takes `role` from
      the database rather than the token, so a demotion applies immediately.
    
    Also fixed: the backend build silently produced an empty dist/
    
    nest-cli deleteOutDir wipes dist/, but tsconfig stored the incremental
    cache at the package root, where it survived. The next build then
    believed everything was already emitted and exited 0 having written
    nothing — a container built from it would have no entrypoint. Moved
    tsBuildInfoFile inside dist/ in both tsconfig.json and
    tsconfig.build.json (the latter overrides compilerOptions, so it needs
    its own). Verified by building twice in a row.
    
    Tests: 11 cases in token-revocation.service.spec.ts covering per-token
    and per-user revocation, the same-second edge case, cross-user isolation,
    TTL scoping, fail-closed behaviour, and legacy tokens with no jti.
    Updated email-verification.service.spec.ts for the new dependency.
    Backend auth suite 66 -> 87 passing; the 22 pre-existing failures are
    unchanged.

