commit add9abe24bf18dae5c0e9a030492c30c7eca62c1
Author: Avijit Pal <palavijit713130@gmail.com>
Date:   Sat Aug 8 12:59:54 2026 +0000

    fix(auth): verify OAuth CSRF state server-side and stop putting the JWT in the URL
    
    Two CRITICAL vulnerabilities in the Google sign-in flow.
    
    1. Login CSRF / account takeover
       The strategy set `state: false` and nothing verified state on the way
       back. The frontend generated a state and compared it in the callback,
       but the backend never echoed one, so the comparison always ran against
       null and the check could never pass — protection in appearance only.
    
       Exploit: an attacker completes Google consent with their own account,
       captures the resulting /auth/google/callback URL, and gets a victim to
       load it. With no state bound to the victim's session the backend
       accepts it and issues a session for the ATTACKER's account. The victim
       then writes journals, media and personal notes into an account the
       attacker controls and can read at will.
    
    2. Access token exposed in a query string
       The callback redirected to /auth/callback?token=<JWT> with a live
       15-minute bearer token. Query strings are recorded in browser history
       and session restore, in CDN/proxy/Cloudflare access logs, and leak via
       Referer to anything the callback page loads. Commit 5911a6b had
       already fixed this; 3da089c reverted it.
    
    Fix
    
    - New OAuthStateService owns both halves in Redis: a 300s CSRF state and
      a 30s single-use authorization code. Both are consumed with GETDEL so
      they can never be redeemed twice, with a GET+DEL fallback for Redis
      < 6.2.
    - New GoogleOAuthGuard mints the state before redirecting to Google.
      Passport's own state store stays disabled because it needs
      express-session, which conflicts with this stateless JWT setup — that
      path was already tried and abandoned in 0973b23 / f17d53b / 9d900b5.
      Keying is by opaque UUID, not client IP, which is unreliable behind
      Cloudflare.
    - The callback now verifies and consumes the state BEFORE issuing any
      credential and redirects to /auth?error=invalid_state on failure.
    - The redirect carries a single-use code instead of the token. The SPA
      POSTs it to the existing /auth/exchange, which was already implemented
      and routed but orphaned: only the writer had been deleted.
    - Removed the client-generated `?state=` from the sign-in button. It
      would have overridden the server's value and re-broken the check.
    - The frontend guards the exchange with a ref so StrictMode's double
      effect invocation cannot burn the single-use code.
    
    Hardening found along the way
    
    - /auth/exchange took `@Body('code') code: string`, which bypasses the
      global ValidationPipe entirely (no metatype to validate). An unbounded
      attacker-controlled string was interpolated straight into a Redis key.
      Now an ExchangeCodeDto with @IsUUID('4').
    - exchangeCode did GET then DEL, leaving a window where two concurrent
      requests could each mint a session from one code. Now atomic, and it
      validates the decoded payload instead of trusting JSON.parse.
    - finishOAuthLogin returned its refresh token through an `as any` cast on
      AuthResponseDto, which does not declare the field — one careless return
      away from serialising a refresh token to a client and defeating
      httpOnly. Replaced with an explicit InternalAuthResult type.
    
    Tests: 10 cases in oauth-state.service.spec.ts covering state round-trip,
    rejection of never-issued state (the CSRF case), single-use enforcement,
    the GETDEL fallback, corrupt payloads and TTL bounds. Backend auth suite
    goes 66 -> 76 passing; the 22 pre-existing failures are unchanged and
    untouched by this commit.

