commit eb77b1e99723e1e77f90b917e6c1fec75d9e979f
Author: Avijit Pal <palavijit713130@gmail.com>
Date:   Sat Aug 8 09:38:05 2026 +0000

    fix(api): correct auth refresh and retry semantics in apiFetch
    
    Every mutation issued with an expired access token failed with
    "Network error. Please check your connection." while the write was
    silently discarded. Nine interacting defects; all invisible to tsc and
    ESLint, so this lands with regression tests.
    
    1. Mutations died after a successful refresh. maxRetries was 0 for
       POST/PATCH/DELETE, so the 401 branch refreshed the token, hit
       "continue", exited the loop and fell through to
       NetworkError('Max retries exceeded'). Transport retries (unsafe to
       replay) and auth retries (always safe - a 401 means the server
       rejected the request, so it had no effect) are now separate counters.
    
    2. Retry predicates tested "retries" instead of the method-aware bound,
       so a genuinely failed POST slept 1s and then discarded the real error.
    
    3. NetworkError erased the underlying failure. It now carries a cause.
    
    4. clearTimeout fired before the response body was read, so a server
       that sent headers then stalled the body hung forever with no timer
       armed. Moved into a finally block.
    
    5. The 401 path called setAccessToken(null) before refreshing, which
       under concurrency nulled a token a winning refresh had just stored
       and burned an extra refresh-token rotation.
    
    6. getValidToken swallowed refresh failures and sent the request
       unauthenticated, guaranteeing a second 401 and a second refresh. It
       now clears the session and emits AUTH_EXPIRED_EVENT once; __root.tsx
       listens and redirects to /auth?next=... instead of leaving the SPA
       logged-in-looking while every request 401s.
    
    7. isTokenExpired used atob on base64url payloads. Any - or _ or
       non-ASCII claim threw, and the catch returned "expired", forcing a
       refresh before every request. Now decodes base64url properly and
       fails open (let the server judge).
    
    8. anySignal attached an abort listener per attempt to the caller's
       long-lived signal and never detached, leaking closures for the life
       of a React Query subscription. It now returns a release() called in
       a finally block.
    
    9. apiPost/apiPatch dropped falsy bodies (0, empty string, false) by
       testing truthiness; now they test against undefined. apiUpload
       clobbered caller headers with an empty object; it now preserves them
       and only strips Content-Type so the browser can set the multipart
       boundary.
    
    Also: /auth/logout authenticates via the httpOnly refresh cookie, so it
    now passes skipAuth - previously logging out with an expired token
    triggered a refresh (rotating the refresh token) merely to end the
    session. /auth/logout-all is behind JwtAuthGuard and correctly still
    sends the bearer token; the 401 skip-list matches exact paths so the
    two are not conflated. refreshAccessToken gained its own
    AbortController, since a hung /auth/refresh previously blocked every
    queued request indefinitely.
    
    Tests: 8 cases in src/lib/api/fetch.test.ts. Verified they fail against
    the previous implementation (3 failures) and pass against this one.

